{"title":"1992 AJHSME Problems/Problem 19","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1992_AJHSME_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The distance between the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/f/3cf9e9d48653308c5beea20eaa99c4457242c580.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^\\text{th}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/6/96695c3b995eea743607b8c269ac180506466150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$26^\\text{th}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> exits on an interstate highway is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/0/a006c697632ebcf73bf31ecf61afa52d9118fd14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$118$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> miles.  If any two consecutive exits are at least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> miles apart, then what is the largest number of miles there can be between two consecutive exits that are between the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/f/3cf9e9d48653308c5beea20eaa99c4457242c580.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5^\\text{th}$\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/6/96695c3b995eea743607b8c269ac180506466150.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$26^\\text{th}$\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> exits?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/f/cbf95c70e91233f7cf59274f16bce2ca6aff11af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{(A)}\\ 8 \\qquad \\text{(B)}\\ 13 \\qquad \\text{(C)}\\ 18 \\qquad \\text{(D)}\\ 47 \\qquad \\text{(E)}\\ 98$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"366\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1992_AJHSME_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/5/f25d31faf472ddb4c698a14522fe8125c06f8dd8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$21$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> pairs of consecutive exits. To find the maximum number of miles of one of these, the other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> must be equal to the minimum number yielding a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/0/200fc40decd50e6749a706753b52b265a7e25f8b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5)(20)=100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"106\" height=\"18\" /> miles. The longest distance must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/1/5916eb239653537a16082740a6dd43013714386c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$118-100=\\boxed{\\text{(C)}\\ 18}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"161\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1992_AJHSME_Problems/Problem_19&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1992_AJHSME\" title=\"1992 AJHSME\">1992 AJHSME</a></b> (<b>